WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:22.100
To look over the horizon and wonder what's
there. It's part of what makes us human. Even

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:28.210
before we were fully human. Some 2 million
years ago, our early ancestors, first left

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:37.110
Africa for Asia, long before the age of European
exploration, ancient Polynesians in canoes

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:44.760
spread throughout the vast Pacific
Phoenicians mastered the Mediterranean and

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:53.820
Vikings crossed the Atlantic
500 years ago in 1519 Magellan set forth on

00:00:53.820 --> 00:01:01.780
the first successful voyage to circumnavigate
the entire Globe and in the 20th century,

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:07.930
humans reached the planet's highest peaks
and its distant Poles

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:14.560
With the invention of the airplane. A new
era of exploration was launched

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:20.181
And about a generation after Lindbergh flew
the Atlantic, we reached outer space and a

00:01:20.181 --> 00:01:28.010
vast new frontier. It was 50 years ago this
summer that Apollo 11 emerged from a ball

00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:37.360
of fire, streaking towards the moon with three
men on board, Neil Armstrong, Buzz Aldrin,

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:42.480
and Michael Collins.
Ladies and gentlemen, it is my pleasure to

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:47.570
introduce it as your pleasure to be in his
presence. The command module pilot for the

00:01:47.570 --> 00:02:10.160
Apollo 11 mission, Mike Collins.
Good to see you pal, how are you? Alright

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:11.780
Mike, It's really, it's a great pleasure to
have you here.

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:15.349
Thank you, Miles. It's nice to be here at
this magnificent festival.

00:02:15.349 --> 00:02:19.860
Miles O’Brien: Yeah, it's a good crowd out
there. You know, I will command every, the

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:24.640
astronauts, uh, on our program today, all
three of them have written books and with

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:29.250
all due respect to the other two astronauts
will be here. Mike wrote the book on writing

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:35.910
books on flying in space. His book carrying
the fire, which is now back out in print.

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:41.930
Is a beautiful, eloquent tribute to space
with great technical detail and a great sense

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:46.340
of, um, what the mission is all about.
So, uh, I recommend you read that and one

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:52.090
of the things you say in your book at the
end, uh, which kind of struck me is a mission

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:59.010
like Apollo 11 is a mission that really never
ends. Here we are 50 years later you're still

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:03.880
talking about it. And, and one of the, it's
a, it's a blessing and a curse in some ways.

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:08.260
You have to answer the same questions over
and over again. So I need to know right up

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:14.790
front, what's your least favorite question
that you get from the likes of me?

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:22.020
What was it really like up there? What was,
what really like up?

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:29.900
So Mike, what was it really like up there?
No, I uh, it's interesting that question like

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:32.680
you've been holding out or something.
Yes.

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:37.620
But having said all that, uh, I know, um,
the three of you each have had a different

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:43.260
approach. And I'm speaking of Neil Armstrong
and Buzz Aldrin. Uh, it, it, there are times

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:47.790
when you prefer not to talk about it and there
are times when you'd like to, um, what's your

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:52.420
feeling 50 years later now that you've had
a chance to put a little time between you

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:58.150
and the mission, how do you feel about reliving
it in some fashion for people like this?

00:03:58.150 --> 00:04:03.739
Well, I like to talk about so many different
facets of, you know, what's it like up there?

00:04:03.739 --> 00:04:10.129
What is, what specifically, but I, I like
to talk I guess a about Neil Armstrong a bit.

00:04:10.129 --> 00:04:17.260
Uh, um, he and Buzz a were remarkable crew
members. They were, they were just wonderful.

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:22.319
Uh, Neil was, um, I don't want to say the
best of the bunch, but he was the best of

00:04:22.319 --> 00:04:29.310
the bunch, we were experimental test pilots.
Uh, and he was a foremost among us, uh, because

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:36.560
of his flying experience with the X-15 rocket
ship, which still today holds speed and altitude

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:43.060
records. He was a very intelligent man. He
had wide, uh, breadth of interests, uh, far

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:49.360
beyond a NASA or the space program, centering
on history, probably the history of science.

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:57.100
But, uh, around, uh, we were very fortunate
after the flight of Apollo 11 to have a around

00:04:57.100 --> 00:05:01.670
the world trip.
And Neil was our, uh, our, uh, spokesperson,

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:07.960
uh, at, for those occasions. And, um, wherever
he went, he was a man of few words, but he

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:13.210
had chosen him very carefully. He knew the
background of the country we were visiting.

00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:22.430
He knew about a lot about their people and
their country. He welcomed, welcomed the audience

00:05:22.430 --> 00:05:29.010
to come with him, aboard the spacecraft and
fly with us. And I, I think I did that successfully.

00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:36.120
And uh, the thing that amazed me about it
was that, uh, I thought the reaction would

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:43.870
be uh, you, you Americans um, finally went
to the moon. Instead of that, it was we did

00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:50.520
it, we humans, we finally got together, we
did something that is wonderful. We can all

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:58.440
embrace it. North, south, east, west, rich,
poor. Everywhere we went, the reaction was

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:05.620
we did it. And I can't think of another achievement
that has brought such unanimity of opinion

00:06:05.620 --> 00:06:11.370
about anything that we humans do here on earth.
It's an amazing irony because it was born

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:17.600
out of cold war rivalry and in the end, instead
of it being, we won, it was like we all did

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:21.460
it. And you'd get congratulations from the
Soviet Union even. That is extraordinary.

00:06:21.460 --> 00:06:26.229
Yes, I believe it is.
I know you talk about this sort of a daisy

00:06:26.229 --> 00:06:32.421
chain of events that have to occur to make
a mission successful. And um, there are any

00:06:32.421 --> 00:06:38.010
number of variables, but in, in many cases
what NASA does is, has wood has redundancy

00:06:38.010 --> 00:06:44.020
or redundancies for redundancies. But I do
know, I think you picked 11 spots along the

00:06:44.020 --> 00:06:50.190
way of Apollo 11 where you were most concerned
and one of them is, um, what we're going to

00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:53.980
see a brief clip of right now. Let's roll
the clip and we'll talk about the significance

00:06:53.980 --> 00:07:00.850
of this moment.
July 19, Apollo 11 slows down and goes into

00:07:00.850 --> 00:07:08.960
orbit around the moon. The bright blue planet
of earth now lives 238,000 miles beyond the

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:15.270
lunar horizon. Astronauts Armstrong and Aldrin,
now in the lunar module, separate from the

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:28.710
command module. Astronaut Collins remains
behind. Preparation for the lunar module descent

00:07:28.710 --> 00:07:37.860
to the moon now begins.
So that moment when the lunar module separated

00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:42.570
from the command module, Columbia, the lunar
module being the Eagle, and your two crew

00:07:42.570 --> 00:07:49.509
mates went off, that was a tense moment, and
those hours when they were on the surface

00:07:49.509 --> 00:07:53.889
were a period of time when you really sweated
the details. Tell us a little bit about what

00:07:53.889 --> 00:07:59.710
was going through your mind and your concerns.
Actually the, a, their separation from me

00:07:59.710 --> 00:08:06.600
in Columbia and their descent to the landing
was not what was really foremost on my mind.

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:12.800
I thought Neil would do a good job. He'd find
a smooth, nice place to put eagle down. And

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:18.310
although he fiddled and diddled a little bit
on some of the terrain he considered to be

00:08:18.310 --> 00:08:23.419
rejectable for one reason or another, he still
found a beautiful landing spot with about

00:08:23.419 --> 00:08:30.729
30 seconds of fuel remaining and put it down.
Um, this I kind of expected, uh, what worried

00:08:30.729 --> 00:08:37.770
me was the return. We, uh, we in NASA enjoyed,
um, machinery that was redundant. Usually

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:43.620
they, we had to have something, uh, not so
in the case of ascent engine, the engine that

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:51.660
would bring, uh, Neil and Buzz from the surface
back up to me coming by 60 miles above them,

00:08:51.660 --> 00:08:59.870
um, that, that just had one, one small little
combustion chamber that had to work properly

00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:06.180
or you, we had two dead man on the moon and
that was, I think what was foremost on my

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:10.860
mind. I had a long list of things to worry
about, but uh, I think that was number one.

00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:18.500
That burn, that moment, pretty key and clearly
the implications of it not working were a

00:09:18.500 --> 00:09:23.350
solo trip back to earth by you. And I assume
you thought a little bit about that.

00:09:23.350 --> 00:09:27.070
Well, you know, I did think a lot about it
and I'm sure Neil and Buzz both thought a

00:09:27.070 --> 00:09:32.250
lot about it. It was definitely not something
we talked about. He, they knew, I knew, uh,

00:09:32.250 --> 00:09:37.850
uh, if, uh, if they couldn't get off for some
reason, a, there was nothing I could do about

00:09:37.850 --> 00:09:43.550
it. I had no landing gear on Columbia. I could
not go down and rescue them. I either committed

00:09:43.550 --> 00:09:49.350
suicide or came home and, uh, and, and of
course I chose to come home in a situation

00:09:49.350 --> 00:09:51.730
like that.
I think that’s a good choice.

00:09:51.730 --> 00:09:57.899
I felt that it'd be a marked a man for life
really if I'd been in that situation.

00:09:57.899 --> 00:10:00.970
What do you mean when you say you'd be a marked
man? What do you think? How do you think it

00:10:00.970 --> 00:10:03.810
would have played out? We don't have to go
too far down that road, but I'm curious.

00:10:03.810 --> 00:10:09.620
Well, I, I think people would have been sympathetic
to my situation and they would not have been

00:10:09.620 --> 00:10:13.660
critical of me, but for the rest of my life
they say, oh, that's that guy. You know, who

00:10:13.660 --> 00:10:19.060
left those two people to die on the moon.
Yeah. That, that, that would be tough. That'd

00:10:19.060 --> 00:10:23.640
be tough. Let's, uh, let's go rewind the,
uh, the tape just a little bit and go back

00:10:23.640 --> 00:10:30.950
to your early days at NASA. Uh, you, uh, Air
Force fighter pilot and test pilot came to

00:10:30.950 --> 00:10:36.980
NASA in class number three. You called yourself
the 14, right? Yeah. There were 14 of us.

00:10:36.980 --> 00:10:40.450
So it was the, the first, it was the seven
than the nine, and then the 14. Right. So

00:10:40.450 --> 00:10:47.180
this is as a ramped up toward, um, Apollo
and, um, a lot of diversity in that shot wouldn’t

00:10:47.180 --> 00:10:53.560
you say? What do you, what do you think? A
lot of white male test pilots in that picture,

00:10:53.560 --> 00:10:56.930
you might say.
Oh, oh yes. Yeah, no, no, I quite agree. And

00:10:56.930 --> 00:11:01.670
then that seems like a, an aberration today
and it, and it is, and in the future should

00:11:01.670 --> 00:11:09.860
be an aberration, but, uh, it wasn't really
NASA's fault. It was society's fault. Uh,

00:11:09.860 --> 00:11:15.941
NASA, uh, picked graduates of an experimental
test pilot school, there are are only three

00:11:15.941 --> 00:11:24.151
of those around. Uh, so the population available
to, uh, to NASA was very limited. Uh, for

00:11:24.151 --> 00:11:33.300
example, NASA recently picked, uh, 12 new,
uh, astronaut candidates. Uh, and to narrow

00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:39.029
it down to 12, they started with over 18,000
applications. Uh.

00:11:39.029 --> 00:11:45.590
So do your homework kids.
So totally different than in my time. NASA

00:11:45.590 --> 00:11:51.529
picked graduates of experimental test pilot
schools. At that time, uh, uh, NASA did not

00:11:51.529 --> 00:11:59.090
hire a women to be experimental test pilots.
Uh, there were one or two black candidates

00:11:59.090 --> 00:12:05.220
around, but you know, in the flood of applications,
they, they kinda got lost in it somehow. So

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:12.209
that's what you ended up with. It was, however,
I defend NASA in saying that they were not

00:12:12.209 --> 00:12:17.670
on purpose selecting white males, that was
our society in those years.

00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:23.149
So the decision to pick test pilots, I guess
that makes sense. But they could've gone in

00:12:23.149 --> 00:12:26.079
other directions, couldn't they?
Yes, of course. And they have gone another

00:12:26.079 --> 00:12:30.839
directions and I'm, I'm glad, they should
go in other directions. We were more concerned

00:12:30.839 --> 00:12:36.180
with the up and the down getting these new
machines into the air successfully, uh, testing

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:43.170
them, uh, bringing them back down to earth.
And now that we've gone past that stage and

00:12:43.170 --> 00:12:47.769
we're into, what are we going to do up there?
What's the science? What's going on? Why do

00:12:47.769 --> 00:12:53.260
people want to go, uh, what can they learn?
And that really has got very little to do

00:12:53.260 --> 00:12:58.110
with the upping and the downing that so obsesses
the test pilot.

00:12:58.110 --> 00:13:02.370
So this I believe is a picture of you and
some of your class doing survival training

00:13:02.370 --> 00:13:07.021
because you know, God forbid if there was
what we would call a downrange abort or a

00:13:07.021 --> 00:13:11.709
landing in a location that wasn't the ocean
near an aircraft carrier, you might have to

00:13:11.709 --> 00:13:16.230
survive for a few days in a place like the
jungle. And I know you had the great opportunity

00:13:16.230 --> 00:13:21.110
to um, camp out and eat Iguana. Is that right?
How was it?

00:13:21.110 --> 00:13:27.300
Just like chicken.
What was the, was the training, how much of

00:13:27.300 --> 00:13:32.190
the training did you think was kind of nutty
and how much of it was worthwhile?

00:13:32.190 --> 00:13:43.080
Well, none of it was out and out nutty. Uh,
some of it was, uh, had been given a great

00:13:43.080 --> 00:13:50.180
deal of thought in, in the sense of, suppose
this goes wrong or this gadget breaks. And

00:13:50.180 --> 00:13:55.600
uh, and that was what we spent a great deal
of our time doing. Failure mode, we called

00:13:55.600 --> 00:14:00.820
it, um, but you can take failure modes to
an extreme. You know what If this does, well

00:14:00.820 --> 00:14:05.120
then you fix that. But while you're fixing
that, the other thing goes wrong. Well, we

00:14:05.120 --> 00:14:09.139
kind of had a real, well, we just can't go
there. We're going to only pick one horrible

00:14:09.139 --> 00:14:13.470
thing at a time and not try to bunch them
all up together.

00:14:13.470 --> 00:14:16.540
And inevitably the thing that goes wrong is
the thing you didn't think about, right?

00:14:16.540 --> 00:14:21.649
That's, yeah. What, what worries you most
about a flight? You hit the nail on the head

00:14:21.649 --> 00:14:25.600
it's that thing that you overlooked in your
training. That's what we worried about.

00:14:25.600 --> 00:14:29.601
So let's talk about your first mission in
Space Gemini 10. And by the way, do you to

00:14:29.601 --> 00:14:32.970
pronounce the Gemini or Gemini? I've heard
it in the old days. We used to say Gemini.

00:14:32.970 --> 00:14:36.400
I hear Gemini. What are you going?
No I, Monday, Wednesday and Friday. I do Gemini

00:14:36.400 --> 00:14:42.459
and Tuesday Thursday I go Gemini.
Gemini or Gemini, whatever you prefer. And

00:14:42.459 --> 00:14:49.470
I, the Great John Young was your, your crewmate
on that one who ultimately flew in Gemini,

00:14:49.470 --> 00:14:55.269
Apollo and the Shuttle Program for many years
flew six shuttle missions and amazing career

00:14:55.269 --> 00:15:00.890
at NASA. Uh, but your mission on Gemini 10,
you had two spacewalks, you're the first guy

00:15:00.890 --> 00:15:08.130
to do that. Uh, and um, as I understand it,
there are, we don't have many pictures of

00:15:08.130 --> 00:15:11.399
that spacewalk. Tell us what happened when
you were out there.

00:15:11.399 --> 00:15:20.440
Uh, I think you say I lost my camera. Is that
what you're alluding to? Details, details.

00:15:20.440 --> 00:15:24.810
This was the best selfies ever, potentially.
And there they are, they're still floating

00:15:24.810 --> 00:15:30.149
around maybe, who knows right?
Who knows, no, my, my job was to leave the

00:15:30.149 --> 00:15:39.760
Gemini uh, um, John was flying it in formation
below, fairly close to it, maybe 20, 25 feet

00:15:39.760 --> 00:15:44.070
away. And I had a little maneuver unit, a
thing called, we call it a gun cause it sort

00:15:44.070 --> 00:15:49.139
of looked like a little pistol you squirted
gas out of that and that allowed you to propel

00:15:49.139 --> 00:15:55.470
yourself. And so I had to propel myself over
to the Ageana, our target vehicle that had

00:15:55.470 --> 00:16:01.019
been up there for months and uh, and from
it, uh, bring back an experiment package and

00:16:01.019 --> 00:16:08.209
return it to the Gemini and that eventually
I did. But I had all kinds of things which

00:16:08.209 --> 00:16:17.490
I indelicately call maneuvers ass over tea
kettle, uh, at the end of a 50 foot, uh, umbilical

00:16:17.490 --> 00:16:23.380
before I was the second time able to get to
where the package was and bring it ok back

00:16:23.380 --> 00:16:28.510
into the Gemini. Without a camera!
And the camera, there it all went. That must

00:16:28.510 --> 00:16:32.360
have been slightly disappointing. Of course,
having done the space walk probably made that

00:16:32.360 --> 00:16:39.139
a little easier to go get through. Let's talk
a little bit about, you know, we know so much

00:16:39.139 --> 00:16:43.570
now, 50 years later, how little was known
about what you were going to accomplish. You

00:16:43.570 --> 00:16:49.120
had to rely on all kinds of simulations. We
have a picture here of, I believe it's Buzz

00:16:49.120 --> 00:16:53.600
and Neil practicing gathering rocks on the
surface of the moon. And I know you spent

00:16:53.600 --> 00:16:57.510
a lot of time learning geology that you wish
you didn't have to spend time doing. But I

00:16:57.510 --> 00:17:02.779
do know this, uh, the number of simulations
you did were extremely challenging. What do

00:17:02.779 --> 00:17:09.040
you are talking after all about 1969 with
key punches, you know, cards going into computers,

00:17:09.040 --> 00:17:13.740
trying to run simulators, simulators that
would crash and so forth. When it came, as

00:17:13.740 --> 00:17:19.929
you got closer to that July 16th date for
launch, which was set by virtue of what the

00:17:19.929 --> 00:17:24.859
lighting conditions were going to be at the
landing site and you had to launch in that

00:17:24.859 --> 00:17:29.039
time or wait another month. Did you guys feel
like you were ready?

00:17:29.039 --> 00:17:38.450
Um, I, I'm guessing that if you gave a crew
six months, which is what we had, or a year,

00:17:38.450 --> 00:17:43.669
which most of them had, or two years, which
some of them had or even longer and later

00:17:43.669 --> 00:17:51.130
on years. Um, I suppose that a, irrespective
of that, the crew would say, well, you know,

00:17:51.130 --> 00:17:57.779
I had this notebook, I got 89 things checked
off, but I still got 22 to go. The, that you

00:17:57.779 --> 00:18:04.840
never, I don't think fully 100% confident
that you know everything about what is going

00:18:04.840 --> 00:18:12.740
to happen or what might happen or might not
happen. Uh, so I think, I think most people

00:18:12.740 --> 00:18:17.960
would feel like they could use a little extra
time and I'm sure we did also.

00:18:17.960 --> 00:18:22.059
Well, it's hard to simulate something that
hasn't happened before. You have to, there's

00:18:22.059 --> 00:18:25.729
a lot of guessing that goes along with that.
Right. In the end do you think the training

00:18:25.729 --> 00:18:29.830
match pretty well?
The simulators were extremely accurate based

00:18:29.830 --> 00:18:36.010
on what we knew ahead of time. Uh, some of
their presentations, what you saw out the

00:18:36.010 --> 00:18:42.630
window, we're not good at all. They were very
crude. But in terms of the duplication of

00:18:42.630 --> 00:18:49.030
the instrument panels and more important than
that, uh, the software that backed up how

00:18:49.030 --> 00:18:56.590
those instruments would record good events,
bad events, questionable events, that aspect

00:18:56.590 --> 00:19:04.600
of the simulator training was, was crucial
to us. And, and, uh, hats off to all the engineers

00:19:04.600 --> 00:19:08.629
who put the long hours into designing those
things.

00:19:08.629 --> 00:19:14.289
So when you think about what you did, the
millions of parts, millions of subsystems,

00:19:14.289 --> 00:19:19.549
all from the low bidder that gave you the
Saturn five and the stack which got you to

00:19:19.549 --> 00:19:27.400
the moon and back, even if it's highly effective
and efficient and, and works as planned, even

00:19:27.400 --> 00:19:33.129
a small percentage that still gives you thousands
of possible failures with that many systems.

00:19:33.129 --> 00:19:39.679
Do you still marvel at how well it worked?
I do indeed. Uh, uh, those machines look smooth

00:19:39.679 --> 00:19:46.240
and nice on the outside, inside they can,
uh, be kind of a nightmare. I think in the,

00:19:46.240 --> 00:19:52.179
uh, command module a design of one switch,
one switch alone, I believe I had 300 of those

00:19:52.179 --> 00:19:57.309
switches. Uh, presumably I should know each
and every detail about every one of the 300.

