uh, to add those to the, uh, federally appropriated,
uh, Kitty and you can do more with more than

00:56:47.530 --> 00:56:53.619
you can with less and I, I welcome aboard.
I think it's wonderful.

00:56:53.619 --> 00:57:00.950
What do you guys think? Scott would you have
any hesitation flying on whether it's a, a,

00:57:00.950 --> 00:57:05.869
you know, a Blue Origin craft or a SpaceX
craft to the space station or perhaps beyond?

00:57:05.869 --> 00:57:10.440
Would you do it?
I would have hesitation flying on anything.

00:57:10.440 --> 00:57:18.230
Which is appropriate, right?
It is very risky. Um, uh, you know, as we

00:57:18.230 --> 00:57:25.500
get more experience, it becomes less risky.
So, uh, I would want to learn about the rocket,

00:57:25.500 --> 00:57:31.020
understand the systems, the redundancy, but
I think, you know, I could, I would probably,

00:57:31.020 --> 00:57:38.099
uh, having done that, absolutely fly on a
Blue Origin or SpaceX rocket. I mean they,

00:57:38.099 --> 00:57:43.410
I've been to Blue Origin, I'd been to SpaceX.
They, uh, those two companies, both seem very,

00:57:43.410 --> 00:57:49.099
very professional, very talented people. But,
um, yeah, I wouldn't just jump on without

00:57:49.099 --> 00:57:52.779
understanding it because I want to come back.
I like Earth.

00:57:52.779 --> 00:57:57.630
I'm just going to say we're not building this
in a vacuum, but that, that has a different

00:57:57.630 --> 00:58:03.460
context. And, you know, we, we are, uh, you
know, as people, we're standing on shoulders

00:58:03.460 --> 00:58:06.799
of giants. They’re sitting on this stage
with me. Um, believe it or not, I'm having

00:58:06.799 --> 00:58:12.460
a hard time believing it, but, um, it's, we
have, we worked very closely with NASA. We

00:58:12.460 --> 00:58:19.640
take a lot of lessons learned from decades
past. Uh, at Blue. We have a really nice mix

00:58:19.640 --> 00:58:23.270
of, um, veterans who've been in the industry
for decades.

00:58:23.270 --> 00:58:27.440
NASA astronauts.
NASA astronauts. We've got three veteran NASA

00:58:27.440 --> 00:58:33.160
astronauts, ah, working at Blue, including
the head of the New Shepard program. Um, so

00:58:33.160 --> 00:58:37.029
we, and we also have those that are coming
out of, out of university. So we've got this

00:58:37.029 --> 00:58:41.769
really nice balance because we really, really
do believe that this company and these, these

00:58:41.769 --> 00:58:48.489
projects are going to be around for a while.
So we, we are building a system that's going

00:58:48.489 --> 00:58:53.230
to be the, the workhorse for the American
space industry for decades to come. But the

00:58:53.230 --> 00:58:57.819
only way that we can do that is with really
good relationships with NASA and with, uh,

00:58:57.819 --> 00:59:00.180
with those that have done it in the past.
Yeah.

00:59:00.180 --> 00:59:07.529
Leland, how about you? Any hesitations?
I think the, uh, the rewards outweigh the

00:59:07.529 --> 00:59:12.369
risk. When I, when I went to space on the
shuttle, you know, it was the lowest bidder,

00:59:12.369 --> 00:59:15.739
right? I got there and I undid my seatbelt
and I floated over to the window and I looked

00:59:15.739 --> 00:59:20.700
out and I was looking at the Caribbean ocean
and I needed new definitions of the color

00:59:20.700 --> 00:59:27.019
blue to describe what I saw. And that also
fundamentally changed me. And if we can get

00:59:27.019 --> 00:59:33.769
more people to share that experience back,
you know, back to the planet, you'll get more

00:59:33.769 --> 00:59:39.989
energy around exploring. You'll get more kids
excited, you'll get more people doing things,

00:59:39.989 --> 00:59:45.220
working together as one humanity, and that's
how it's worth it to me to take the risk.

00:59:45.220 --> 00:59:50.869
Mike, I know when you were orbiting the moon,
uh, you radioed down at one point, or maybe

00:59:50.869 --> 00:59:55.130
you were on your way back, but you said something
to the effect, I wish, you know, several hundred

00:59:55.130 --> 01:00:00.670
million Americans could have this experience
with me. How do you think that would change

01:00:00.670 --> 01:00:04.289
life on earth?
I don't know. I don't think he even, I don't

01:00:04.289 --> 01:00:11.079
think you have to go to the moon. I'd go up,
a hundred miles is a nice, uh, orbital. I

01:00:11.079 --> 01:00:15.789
mean, you're above all that friction stuff
there and uh, you, you'd be, you got the,

01:00:15.789 --> 01:00:21.720
uh, the communist and the capitalist and a,
they're side by side looking out a window.

01:00:21.720 --> 01:00:27.089
Hell, they can't even find their own country
much less say that one is better than that

01:00:27.089 --> 01:00:36.130
one. Uh, I, I think it would in subtle ways
really change the way political leaders think

01:00:36.130 --> 01:00:41.851
about their, their own turf and other people's,
if it, if you can divide turf into, theirs

01:00:41.851 --> 01:00:48.150
and ours, uh, how you, how you deal with things
on a unified global basis. I think it would

01:00:48.150 --> 01:00:51.420
help.
Yeah. My, my uh, my guy called my Russian

01:00:51.420 --> 01:00:58.119
brother from another mother, Mikhail Kornienko,
Misha, my um, guy I spent nearly a year in

01:00:58.119 --> 01:01:03.569
space. He would sometimes say on the space
station when we were there, he would say,

01:01:03.569 --> 01:01:09.349
you know, if our two countries wanted to solve,
uh, any differences, solve the problems we

01:01:09.349 --> 01:01:19.599
have with one another. All we need to do is
put our two presidents in space together for

01:01:19.599 --> 01:01:22.890
a year.
Leland, this idea, it's been written about

01:01:22.890 --> 01:01:27.560
quite a bit. This idea of, it's called the
overview effect. There is some, something

01:01:27.560 --> 01:01:34.249
that changes when people have this ability.
Is that real? What is it about it? Is there

01:01:34.249 --> 01:01:38.329
something that really changes the way you
view life when you get back down on terra

01:01:38.329 --> 01:01:42.660
firma or is it just a fleeting moment in space
and you're back to your everyday life?

01:01:42.660 --> 01:01:47.609
Well, before I went to space, I was driving
down, I was driving from Houston down to Clear

01:01:47.609 --> 01:01:53.420
Lake on highway 45 and this, driving my car
and this guy in this big pickup truck with

01:01:53.420 --> 01:01:58.680
the big mirrors and the dually cuts me off
and I, I go blep blep. I probably say some

01:01:58.680 --> 01:02:02.650
things I probably shouldn't have said. And
then the same thing happened, but this time

01:02:02.650 --> 01:02:12.710
I was driving from Clear Lake to Houston after
flying in space. And I just said to myself,

01:02:12.710 --> 01:02:18.470
dude, you can't hurt me. I've been to space,
and I do feel like, I mean, Frank White would

01:02:18.470 --> 01:02:22.849
a book about this called The Overview Effect.
And it's a cognitive shift that people get

01:02:22.849 --> 01:02:27.570
when they see the planet from this vantage
point. I feel like there was something cognitively

01:02:27.570 --> 01:02:33.599
that changed in me, especially when I was
breaking bread with Peggy and the rest of

01:02:33.599 --> 01:02:39.349
the team over there in the Russian segment.
And it did feel like something, you know,

01:02:39.349 --> 01:02:42.849
fundamentally changed. Now. Did you, did you
feel that?

01:02:42.849 --> 01:02:49.979
Yeah, of course. Um, you know, and I would
describe it, um, a little bit more. You know,

01:02:49.979 --> 01:02:53.650
when you look at the earth, you don't see
any political borders. Uh, when you look at

01:02:53.650 --> 01:02:59.180
the earth from space, not like we expect to
see it on a map, you think of earth in terms

01:02:59.180 --> 01:03:03.970
of, you know, before you flown in space, you
think of how you view it on map with political

01:03:03.970 --> 01:03:09.549
borders. Of course, you know, you could see
those at night, uh, with the lighting in different

01:03:09.549 --> 01:03:14.920
countries, but during the daytime you don't,
you see an incredibly beautiful planet. Uh,

01:03:14.920 --> 01:03:20.740
you know, because you follow the news that
there are a lot of bad things that go on,

01:03:20.740 --> 01:03:26.119
on a daily basis on planet earth. Uh, and
these are problems that are solvable when

01:03:26.119 --> 01:03:31.140
you're on the space station, this $100 billion
space nation, probably one of the hardest

01:03:31.140 --> 01:03:35.849
things we've ever done.
Uh, if we can do that, you recognize we can,

01:03:35.849 --> 01:03:42.229
you know, do other things to help humanity.
The other thing you notice is just how fragile

01:03:42.229 --> 01:03:47.241
the atmosphere is. It looks like a thin film
over the surface of the earth. When we're

01:03:47.241 --> 01:03:52.779
on the ground, you look up, the sky looks
infinite. When you're in space, I remember

01:03:52.779 --> 01:03:57.509
one of my first days in space I was like,
what is that film? And then you realize that

01:03:57.509 --> 01:04:02.670
is our atmosphere, just the, where all the
pollutants start going into. You know, there's

01:04:02.670 --> 01:04:07.809
some parts of the earth almost always covered
with pollution, parts of Asia. I think, you

01:04:07.809 --> 01:04:11.230
know, kids in those countries, if you asked
them what color the sky was, they'd probably

01:04:11.230 --> 01:04:18.569
say brown or, or gray. You know, the rainforest
in South America. I noticed a very, very obvious

01:04:18.569 --> 01:04:24.859
change between my first flight in 1999 my
last in 2016 you know, fires over certain

01:04:24.859 --> 01:04:31.940
parts of the, the continents. You see the
effect of people that have, how people affect

01:04:31.940 --> 01:04:37.720
this planet, which is incredibly beautiful
but also incredibly fragile.

01:04:37.720 --> 01:04:43.681
I think we talk about fragility of the planet,
but I, and I, I hear what you, both of you

01:04:43.681 --> 01:04:49.559
have said about the fragile oasis, but I think
that it's not that the planet is fragile,

01:04:49.559 --> 01:04:54.239
its that we are screwing it up and that we're
going to be burped out and the planet will

01:04:54.239 --> 01:05:03.170
be resilient and fix itself later. So we're
doing it to the planet and doing it to ourselves.

01:05:03.170 --> 01:05:10.300
And it's, uh, its going to be us.
So one of the, really the reason why, uh,

01:05:10.300 --> 01:05:14.119
Blue is so focused on these reusable rockets
that you talk about is to, uh, access the

01:05:14.119 --> 01:05:20.890
resources of space, but it really is to help
earth. So it's bringing the resources down.

01:05:20.890 --> 01:05:26.430
But ultimately part of our grander vision
is how, how do we get heavy industry off the

01:05:26.430 --> 01:05:32.599
planet? How do we get these pollutant, uh,
elements and industries off and maybe use

01:05:32.599 --> 01:05:37.920
earth just for residential and maybe some
light industry space has a lot of, it might

01:05:37.920 --> 01:05:44.440
sound far off, but the moment that we're gonna
hit that, that oh, oh gosh, moment, it's going

01:05:44.440 --> 01:05:49.900
to be too late to work on technologies that
are gonna be necessary to get us up there

01:05:49.900 --> 01:05:56.650
and get back in and bring resources back and
get some of these, uh, um, again, infrastructure

01:05:56.650 --> 01:06:01.499
up into space. Help us here on earth. There
should not be a plan B. We've been to every

01:06:01.499 --> 01:06:06.869
single one of the planets and this one by
far is the best. So we got to do what we can

01:06:06.869 --> 01:06:12.520
to keep this one happy and healthy.
Yeah. So Mike, uh, I know you've had similar,

01:06:12.520 --> 01:06:17.710
uh, it's had a similar effect on you and made
you very concerned about the environment.

01:06:17.710 --> 01:06:22.920
Tell us a little bit about how your view of
the earth, that earthrise and being able to

01:06:22.920 --> 01:06:27.940
cover up everything you know, with your thumb,
how that changed you as you lived out the

01:06:27.940 --> 01:06:33.150
last 50 years.
Not, not, hadn't changed me enough. I haven't

01:06:33.150 --> 01:06:39.550
done a particularly good job in the last 50
years of, uh, putting my, uh, my footprints

01:06:39.550 --> 01:06:47.680
or uh, my bank account where my money is,
I mean, where my mouth is. Uh, I think we

01:06:47.680 --> 01:06:52.799
can, I don't think I'm rare in that. I mean,
we, I think we talk a good game. I'm not sure

01:06:52.799 --> 01:06:59.549
we play a good game. Uh, I certainly have
not. I, uh, I, I pursue my own hobbies and

01:06:59.549 --> 01:07:07.119
whatnot without really giving consideration
of a sufficient consideration to, uh, the

01:07:07.119 --> 01:07:12.940
fragility and, and, um, and, and, and of course,
uh, you're absolutely right about the, is

01:07:12.940 --> 01:07:18.750
it really fragile or are we fragile people
living on a rock, not sure that matters. Something

01:07:18.750 --> 01:07:24.660
is fragile system is fragile. And, uh, I,
I certainly worry about that. The word fragility

01:07:24.660 --> 01:07:28.269
just floats in and out of my head whether
I wanted it or not.

01:07:28.269 --> 01:07:31.410
Isn't that what you're writing about right
now though? Tell us a little bit about it.

01:07:31.410 --> 01:07:39.299
Um, well I'm, I'm writing a book number five.
It's a series of essays and, uh, one of them

01:07:39.299 --> 01:07:45.140
touches on what we're talking about which
is world population. What should the world

01:07:45.140 --> 01:07:52.539
population be? Uh, is anyone working on trying
to find an answer to that? I don't think so.

01:07:52.539 --> 01:07:57.890
You know, there are organizations, international
organizations, but, uh, the, they right, uh,

01:07:57.890 --> 01:08:03.029
learned papers that they pass back and forth
between half a dozen people and, uh, and their,

01:08:03.029 --> 01:08:09.420
their, their conclusions don't ever seem to
reach, uh, the politicians. Uh, uh, when we

01:08:09.420 --> 01:08:17.989
flew to the moon, the world population was
2 billion. And uh, now it's, uh, I think around

01:08:17.989 --> 01:08:25.089
eight. Predictions are, is going to fairly
soon be 10. And is that sensible? Is that

01:08:25.089 --> 01:08:34.170
okay? Well, maybe, I don't think so however,
but, but my main point is not whether I think

01:08:34.170 --> 01:08:38.900
it's too big, too small or just right, but
that no one is paying the attention to it

01:08:38.900 --> 01:08:45.950
that nation should pay. And it's an extraordinarily
difficult, uh, uh, problem because we all

01:08:45.950 --> 01:08:52.310
know the diversity of north south one factor
after another. But somehow we have to get

01:08:52.310 --> 01:09:01.130
all these, uh, competitive, uh, countries
together to try to at least plan on where

01:09:01.130 --> 01:09:07.190
are we going, where, what do we want it to
be next fiscal year, 10 fiscal years, whatever.

01:09:07.190 --> 01:09:10.029
I think we should be working the problem and
we're not.

01:09:10.029 --> 01:09:19.029
So we face this time of kind of peril and
promise. The promise is a lot of the excitement

01:09:19.029 --> 01:09:27.190
that we hear here and how space can help us
in ways, but does space at some point become

01:09:27.190 --> 01:09:34.030
frivolous and a waste or will it always be
some means towards solving problems here.

01:09:34.030 --> 01:09:40.859
I think the latter, but I've, you know, I'm,
I might be a bit biased on that, but no, I

01:09:40.859 --> 01:09:46.310
mean you, you are literally looking into infinity,
right? This image that you're looking here,

01:09:46.310 --> 01:09:51.349
the amount of resources that we can, that
we can access and use and enhance, you know,

01:09:51.349 --> 01:09:59.030
if we’re looking at a, ultimately a population
cap we’re walking towards stasis, that's

01:09:59.030 --> 01:10:06.170
sad. Humanity is supposed to expand, is supposed
to evolve. I mean, how, you know, think about

01:10:06.170 --> 01:10:10.800
things just plateauing or even going backwards
that seems, yeah, that just makes me sad.

01:10:10.800 --> 01:10:15.670
I think we will continue to look to space
and what it has to offer for our future.

01:10:15.670 --> 01:10:19.440
Scott, how mportant is space in the picture,
when you think about the problems we have

01:10:19.440 --> 01:10:23.840
here on the ground.
So I, uh, you know, for one, it's a technology

01:10:23.840 --> 01:10:28.920
accelerator. I don't, I don't, you know, I'm
not a believer that if it wasn't for Apollo,

01:10:28.920 --> 01:10:33.469
we wouldn't have cell phones right now or
that we wouldn't have computers. But I think-

01:10:33.469 --> 01:10:36.349
Well space food sticks and Tang for sure.
We've got that.

01:10:36.349 --> 01:10:41.150
And we do have the space ice cream, which
we actually don't have in space believe it

01:10:41.150 --> 01:10:48.010
or not, but uh, yeah. So I, I think, you know,
doing the hardest thing we can do, uh, requires

01:10:48.010 --> 01:10:53.849
us to develop technologies to do that. And
I think that improves our lives here on earth.

01:10:53.849 --> 01:11:01.219
Um, I also agree that we are explorers and
as soon as uh, stop, uh, exploring and we

01:11:01.219 --> 01:11:06.360
look inward, we're probably not going to exist
for very much longer. I think that is part

01:11:06.360 --> 01:11:12.949
of our DNA, uh, to always want to see what
is a over the horizon, whether that's the

01:11:12.949 --> 01:11:19.800
horizon of, uh, of the ocean or the horizon
of the moon or eventually Mars.

01:11:19.800 --> 01:11:25.590
But if you made the argument that everything
I just said was wrong and the only thing we

01:11:25.590 --> 01:11:33.500
got out of space flight is that kids in this
country or around the world were motivated

01:11:33.500 --> 01:11:40.870
to become scientists and engineers to do their
homework, to work in fields that are incredibly

01:11:40.870 --> 01:11:46.349
important to our economy. Incredibly important
to our national security. If the investment

01:11:46.349 --> 01:11:54.860
alone just got us motivated and moving in
a positive direction towards developing scientists

01:11:54.860 --> 01:12:03.110
and engineers I think every penny is well
worth the expense. And the last thing is,

01:12:03.110 --> 01:12:09.901
I spent over 500 days on the International
Space Station. There was no money there. All

01:12:09.901 --> 01:12:14.280
that money was spent on earth, you know, with
people and with high paying jobs, good jobs,

01:12:14.280 --> 01:12:20.300
people that then, you know, go on to pay their
taxes to send their kids to college. So I

01:12:20.300 --> 01:12:24.420
think it's an investment in our future in,
you know, many, many different ways.

01:12:24.420 --> 01:12:31.280
Leland, what'd you go along with that?
I agree with Scott and I just think that this,

01:12:31.280 --> 01:12:36.880
this wiring of us in our DNA to explore it
is one of the biggest things that we do with

01:12:36.880 --> 01:12:42.099
space, whether it's the oceans or space. And
when I was interviewed to become an astronaut,

01:12:42.099 --> 01:12:48.610
there were 2,500 people that applied. I was
one of 25 us astronauts chosen. And right

01:12:48.610 --> 01:12:55.130
before I was chosen, John Young turned to
me and he said, Leland, if we stop exploring,

01:12:55.130 --> 01:13:03.260
we will falter as a civilization. And so I
believe John Young and I know that if we stop

01:13:03.260 --> 01:13:07.941
doing this, we're going to die as a civilization.
The other thing is, you know Scott, you just

01:13:07.941 --> 01:13:12.619
said, I had a chance to talk to a kid when
he was 16 years old at the Jet Propulsion

01:13:12.619 --> 01:13:18.969
Laboratory wearing this blue flight suit,
and I gave a commencement speech at George

01:13:18.969 --> 01:13:24.449
Mason and this young guy walks up to me and
he says, hey, do you remember me? I'm getting

01:13:24.449 --> 01:13:29.630
my PhD in aerospace engineering because you
told me that I could.

01:13:29.630 --> 01:13:39.630
That's one kid. So, if we can do this, maybe
that kid is not going to fly in space, maybe

01:13:39.630 --> 01:13:43.190
they’re going to cure cancer or maybe they're
going to get in some other field in build

01:13:43.190 --> 01:13:50.190
your rockets or something. But this hope,
this inspiration, this leadership is something

01:13:50.190 --> 01:13:55.800
that I think we have to do. Whether wherever
the exploration goes, we must explore.

01:13:55.800 --> 01:14:00.530
Should we go back to the moon? Shall we press
toward Mars? How much does that matter if

01:14:00.530 --> 01:14:05.280
it's about inspiring young people? Is it at,
will any mission do?

01:14:05.280 --> 01:14:11.639
I think, uh, I think going to Mars is showing,
I mean, what President Kennedy said back in

01:14:11.639 --> 01:14:16.270
the day, it's because we do the things that
are hard and we've been to the moon. We've

01:14:16.270 --> 01:14:22.409
demonstrated that. Do we need to have a lunar
base as a, as a launch pad to get us to Mars?

01:14:22.409 --> 01:14:28.280
I don't think so, but if we decide to do this
as a civilization, as a people, let's put

01:14:28.280 --> 01:14:31.520
the money up there and do it and go do it
just like Kennedy did.

01:14:31.520 --> 01:14:34.599
What do you think, moon?
Well there, I think there are probably some

01:14:34.599 --> 01:14:38.820
teenagers in here and I don't know if you
guys are aware of this.

01:14:38.820 --> 01:14:41.719
Stand up, teenagers.
All teenagers stand, yeah! The Mars generation!

01:14:41.719 --> 01:14:53.980
We're glad you're here tonight.
I don't know if you realize this, but you

01:14:53.980 --> 01:15:00.100
guys have never been on the earth with all
of the people. Your whole lives, there has

01:15:00.100 --> 01:15:05.130
always been people in space and I think one
of the things we should make a commitment

01:15:05.130 --> 01:15:11.360
to is never changing that. I think no matter
what we do, and someday we will have to put

01:15:11.360 --> 01:15:16.130
the a International Space Station or at least
parts of it in the, in the Pacific Ocean,

01:15:16.130 --> 01:15:21.030
I think we should make a commitment to always
having people in space and at least that keeps

01:15:21.030 --> 01:15:25.219
us moving in the positive, in a positive direction.
You know, someday we're going to go to Mars.

01:15:25.219 --> 01:15:29.690
I hope I get to see it. I hope you guys get
to do it. Uh, maybe we should go back to the

01:15:29.690 --> 01:15:35.210
moon. I think that's a choice depending on
really about how much money you have to invest.

01:15:35.210 --> 01:15:41.060
But I think the planet needs to make a commitment
to always have people in space. We got a 20

01:15:41.060 --> 01:15:51.150
year record. Don't want to break it.
All right. So I'm just going to go down the

01:15:51.150 --> 01:15:55.469
panel, starting with Ariane. Paint the vision,
50 years from now when we come back and do

01:15:55.469 --> 01:16:01.030
this event again and we'll all be here. What
will we be, where will we be in space? You

01:16:01.030 --> 01:16:07.190
think? What's your prediction?
Uh, I mean I think that um, I think that there

01:16:07.190 --> 01:16:12.170
are going to be a lot more people in space.
I think that's going to be driven by the improvements

01:16:12.170 --> 01:16:18.070
in uh, reusable rockets that are going to
be able to get people and again, you know,

01:16:18.070 --> 01:16:23.449
space stations or other sorts of heavy infrastructure
up there. Um, and I think it's going to, as

01:16:23.449 --> 01:16:29.710
we're seeing here, uh, touch a lot more people's
lives, um, through various elements of space

01:16:29.710 --> 01:16:33.340
technology, whether it's people actually getting
up into space or them being able to benefit

01:16:33.340 --> 01:16:38.199
from the technology that's coming from space.
Will there be millions by 50 years or thousands

01:16:38.199 --> 01:16:41.460
or hundreds?
I don't think necessarily millions. That vision

01:16:41.460 --> 01:16:46.221
of millions of people living and working in
space, you know, is it my grandchildren? Is

01:16:46.221 --> 01:16:50.800
it your grandchildren or their grandchildren?
We'll see. We'll see. And in fact, I think

01:16:50.800 --> 01:16:56.040
frankly, the future is, is to be written by
them, right? So we can predict all day, but

01:16:56.040 --> 01:17:00.179
it's the young people in the audience and
the future generations that are gonna make,

01:17:00.179 --> 01:17:03.179
that are gonna paint the picture.
All right, Leland, 50 years from now.

01:17:03.179 --> 01:17:08.220
50 years from now, I'm inspired. These students
who stood up, these kids who stood up, a lot

01:17:08.220 --> 01:17:15.760
of these kids don't see race and don't see
color and don't see isms. So I am very hopeful

01:17:15.760 --> 01:17:20.920
that in 50 years some of those kids will be
orbiting Mars, working together as close knit

01:17:20.920 --> 01:17:26.040
teams with their friends and family and people
from all around the planet. Um, I think it's

01:17:26.040 --> 01:17:32.770
possible if we as a society decide that this
is important. And you know, my experience

01:17:32.770 --> 01:17:39.199
on the space station working with people from
around the world, it, it changed me and I

01:17:39.199 --> 01:17:44.370
think they're already changed in the things
that they do and the tweeting and texting

01:17:44.370 --> 01:17:51.239
and IG-ing and all of this connectivity even
though sometimes a little too much, but um,

01:17:51.239 --> 01:17:56.000
but they will make it there, and we'll be
on Mars whether it's on the planet or orbiting

01:17:56.000 --> 01:17:57.780
it's Mars.
Scott.

01:17:57.780 --> 01:18:05.190
Well, I agree mostly with what my two colleagues
here just said. And if you consider the fact

01:18:05.190 --> 01:18:12.640
that we went from flying an airplane for the
